{
  "name": "Room grid",
  "description": "Six cells in two columns, filled row-major, so the frame is a three by two grid of rooms whatever the item count: a list of four leaves the last two cells empty rather than stretching four rooms over six boxes. The large tile lays the same row out differently and hides the name line entirely, which is what per-family placement of a ROW layer means: a row layer is keyed in the placements map by its own id, exactly like a top-level layer, and the resolver applies the shape's placement to it before anything is drawn.",
